<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aleut</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Unangan, Aleut people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aleut, or Unangan, are the Indigenous people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleutian-islands">Aleutian Islands</a> and the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> Peninsula, skilled sea hunters in skin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayak">kayaks</a>. From the 1740s Russian fur traders forced them to hunt sea otters, and violence and disease greatly reduced their numbers. Many converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Christianity</a>, which remains strong among them.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aleut.jpg" alt="Aleut" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Malcolm Greany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greany_Attu_Woman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Archaeological evidence indicates that the Aleuts first occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleutian-islands">Aleutian Islands</a> about 9,000 years ago, beginning with the eastern islands, where sites on Unalaska and Anagula date to that period. They then spread westward, and the oldest sites on the western islands begin to appear about 2,500 years ago. Their technologies centred on food and resources from the sea and coast: fishing, gathering marine invertebrates and hunting sea mammals. Tools were made from local obsidian and chert, bird bones for needles and awls, and mammal bones for wedges, spoons and harpoon points. Before sustained European contact about 25,000 Aleuts lived in the archipelago.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 18th century Russian traders, the promyshlenniki, set up settlements on the islands, driven by demand for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> the Aleuts hunted. They took Aleut women and children hostage to force the men to hunt foxes and sea otters, and often enslaved Aleut men as well. The Aleuts also suffered heavy deaths in the 19th and early 20th centuries from Eurasian infectious diseases to which they had no immunity, and their customary ways of life were disrupted. Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> missionaries reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodiak-island">Kodiak</a> in 1794 and many Aleuts became Christian; Saint Peter the Aleut is counted among the earliest Christian martyrs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><p>In the 1820s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-american-company">Russian-American Company</a>, which administered much of the North Pacific, moved many Aleut families to the Commander Islands and to the Pribilof Islands, which still have majority-Aleut communities. The islands are now divided politically between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s Kamchatka Krai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Aleuts call themselves Unangan in the eastern dialect and Unangas in the western, terms translated as 'original people'. Their language, Unangam Tunuu, belongs to the Eskaleut family and was written in Cyrillic from 1829 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> script from 1870. An estimated 150 people in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and five in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> speak it, and there are revitalisation efforts.</p><p>Aleuts traditionally lived in barabaras, partly underground houses roofed with driftwood, grass and earth, which sheltered several families from wind, rain and cold. They fish for salmon, cod, crabs and shellfish and hunt seals, walrus and whales, drying, smoking or roasting the catch, and many still eat locally sourced foods. The 2000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 11,941 people who identified as Aleut and 17,000 more with partial Aleut ancestry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aleuts” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Many find the older umbrella term Eskimo offensive or forced on them in a colonial way, while the related Yupik and <b>Aleut</b> (Unangan) peoples generally do not call themselves Inuit.</blockquote><a href="/culture/inuit">Inuit · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Aleut</b> and Alutiiq hunters were forced to hunt sea otters, whose pelts were sold to China.</blockquote><a href="/state/russian-america">Russian America</a></li></ul></section>
